import PageSubtitle from "../PageSubtitle.astro";
import LowSeaLevelMap from "./LowSeaLevelMap.astro";
import SkyIslandDiagram from "../svg/SkyIslandDiagram.astro";
import SulawesiAncestryMap from "./SulawesiAncestryMap.astro";
// import ElevationRanges from "./ElevationRanges.astro";

const firstParagraphClasses = cntl`
  first-letter:text-7xl
  first-letter:font-semibold
  first-letter:mr-2
  first-letter:float-left
`;

const spaceClasses = cntl`
  mr-1
`;

const eyebrowClasses = cntl`
  mt-6
  text-xs
  font-semibold
  uppercase
  tracking-wider
  text-moss-700
  dark:text-moss-300
`;
---

<div>
  <PageSubtitle subtitle="Island Speciation" />

  <SectionHead text="Islands as natural laboratories" />
  <Content>
    <p class={firstParagraphClasses}>
      The Indo-Australian Archipelago (IAA) combines marine barriers, ancient
      paleo-islands, and steep mountains, a system where isolation has
      repeatedly driven diversification. During Pleistocene sea-level lows,
      Sumatra, Java, and Borneo joined mainland Asia as Sundaland, while
      Sulawesi, the Moluccas, and the Lesser Sundas stayed isolated by deep
      water. Within islands, mountains act as sky islands. Many of these
      regions remain poorly surveyed. I ask how geographic isolation shapes
      genomic divergence, ecological interactions, and phenotypic evolution.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Pleistocene low sea-level boundaries</p>
  <div class="mt-2 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <LowSeaLevelMap />
  </div>

  <SectionHead text="In situ diversification of the Bunomys clade on Sulawesi" />
  <Content>
    <Image
      src={lenomys}
      alt="Lenomys meyeri, an endemic rat of Sulawesi"
      width={340}
      class="float-left mr-4 mt-2 rounded-xl"
    />
    <p>
      My main study system is the most speciose rodent clade on Sulawesi:
      the <i>Bunomys</i> clade. I study its systematics and in situ
      diversification.
      Integrating morphology, multi-marker Sanger sequencing, and
      ultra-conserved elements, we revealed taxonomic problems in the group and
      a complex interplay between paleo-islands, tectonic plates, and mountain
      formation in promoting its diversification, with mixed support for the
      Area of Endemism paradigm on Sulawesi.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      This work rests on 13 years of collaborative field expeditions across
      more than 20 remote mountain systems in Southeast Asia: over 20
      field-collecting expeditions contributing more than 8,000 voucher
      specimens and 30,000 specimen parts to natural history collections.
      Together with collaborators, this fieldwork has contributed to the
      description or revision of more than 24 mammal species. Beyond
      <i>Bunomys</i>, I support collaborative studies of shrew diversification
      on Sulawesi, Java, and Sumatra. I received the 2023 Murie Family
      Conservation Award from the American Society of Mammalogists for the
      significance of this work in conserving mammals and their habitats.
    </p>
  </Content>

  <div class="clear-both">
    <SectionHead text="An island of islands" />
  </div>
  <Content>
    <p>
      On Sulawesi, ancient paleo-islands, colliding tectonic plates, and young
      mountains together shaped the <i>Bunomys</i> radiation. Above about
      1,000 m, many species are confined to a single mountain, while others
      range from the lowlands to the summits and connect them.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Four sky islands in the central core</p>
  <div class="mt-2 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <SkyIslandDiagram />
  </div>

  {/* Elevational distribution plot hidden for now.
  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Across elevations</p>
  <Content>
    <p>
      Across the whole clade the pattern holds. More than half of the
      species and haplogroups we sampled are montane, most known from a single
      mountain. Widespread taxa cross the 1,000 m threshold, and a few, such
      as <i>T. dominator</i> and <i>B. penitus</i> WC-A, span nearly the whole
      gradient.
      Filter by range class or area of endemism to explore them.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <div class="mt-4 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <ElevationRanges />
  </div>
  */}

  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Across the island</p>
  <Content>
    <p>
      Sulawesi is often divided into seven areas of endemism separated by
      narrow boundary zones. <i>Taeromys dominator</i>, a large endemic rat
      found across the island and from lowlands to high elevations, shows how messy the real system is: one
      ancestry cluster spans mountains in every area of endemism, populations
      in the southeast carry a distinct cluster, and several individuals are
      admixed, so the boundaries are only partly respected.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <div class="mt-4 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <SulawesiAncestryMap />
    <p class="mt-4 text-pretty text-sm text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
      Ancestry of 29 <i>T. dominator</i> from 12 localities, estimated from
      genome-wide SNPs with PopCluster at K = 4, the best-supported K by DLK2. Each pie is one individual;
      individuals from the same locality are fanned out around it. Dark bands
      mark the boundary zones between areas of endemism.
    </p>
  </div>

  <SectionHead text="Parasites and microbiomes: collaborations and next steps" />
  <Content>
    <p>
      The evolutionary history of the <i>Bunomys</i> clade also provides a
      framework for asking whether parasites co-evolve with their hosts or
      cross species boundaries. Work led by <Link
        text="Kevin Rowe's lab at Museums Victoria"
        url="https://museumsvictoria.com.au/about-us/board-and-staff/staff/dr-kevin-rowe/"
      />, Australia, on which I am a co-author, documented native and introduced <i>Trypanosoma</i> lineages
      in Sulawesi rodents, with high prevalence in several <i>Bunomys</i>
      species and infections from village edges to remote montane forests,
      peaking above 2,000 m. The Esselstyn lab studies the gut microbial
      communities of Sulawesi shrews and rodents.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Building on these collaborations, parasite evolution is a direction I
      hope to pursue: integrating host population genomics, parasite
      phylogenies, and environmental data to test whether parasite
      diversification parallels host history or reflects host switching and
      local adaptation. To lay the groundwork, I am building parasite records
      into <Link text="NAHPU" url="https://nahpu.app" />, so parasites
      collected in the field are easier to track and stay associated with
      their host specimens.
    </p>
  </Content>
</div>
